course: dictionary
lesson: 9794
title: Same word, different meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words mean different things in different countries. This is a trap, so look for clues. In America, «suspenders» are straps over the shoulders that hold trousers up. British people call these braces.
explain: The words around a word are its context, and they often tell you the meaning. «He wears blue canvas breeches and suspenders (braces that hold trousers up)» has the meaning right there in the brackets. Always check the sentence before you decide.
explain: In the UK, a suspender is an elastic strap that holds up a stocking or sock. Long ago, women wore suspender belts and men wore sock suspenders. You may see them in old books and photos. Notice too that suspenders is often used in the plural: «a pair of suspenders».
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Suspenders» (US, Canada) means straps over the shoulders that hold trousers up; British people call these braces.
fact: «Suspender» (UK, Ireland, Australia) means an elastic strap that holds up a stocking or sock.
fact: In the past, women wore suspender belts and men wore sock suspenders.
fact: Suspenders is usually used in the plural, as in «a pair of suspenders».
exercise: In «He wears blue canvas breeches and suspenders (braces that hold trousers up)», what are suspenders? | *Braces that hold trousers up | A folding pocket-knife | A hat for the sea | Socks made of wool
exercise: An American says «suspenders». What would a British person call them? | Gloves | *Braces | Stockings | Belts
exercise: What did men wear in the past to hold up their socks? | A pair of gloves | *Sock suspenders | Canvas breeches | A jack-knife
test: Anna, from Chicago, says «my suspenders are broken». What does she mean? | Her favourite shoes | *Her braces that hold her trousers up | Her long woollen scarf | Her elastic straps that hold up her socks
test: In the UK, what is a suspender on a stocking? | *An elastic strap that holds up the stocking | Braces for trousers | A folding pocket-knife | A type of canvas trouser
exam: A British friend says «suspenders» for a stocking strap, but an American friend says it for trousers. Why? | The word is old and no longer used | *The word means different things in different countries | Suspenders are always worn in the plural | Both are wrong, and the word has only one meaning
